WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.267
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:05.133
actor, writer, and director
you know from his work on

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:06.634
"Saturday Night Live,"
"Documentary Now!",

00:00:06.634 --> 00:00:07.734
and "Trainwreck."

00:00:07.734 --> 00:00:10.400
New episodes of his
hit show, "Barry,"

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.934
air Sunday nights on HBO

00:00:11.934 --> 00:00:13.901
and are available to stream
anytime on HBO Max.

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:15.133
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:18.868
-Talking to Sally Reed.
It's spelled R-E-E-D.

00:00:18.868 --> 00:00:21.501
The show's called "Joplin."
I'm talking to Sally Reed.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.634
You all set? Alright.

00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:25.334
Mm.
[ Clears throat ]

00:00:25.334 --> 00:00:26.734
[ Door opens ]
Hey, Sally.

00:00:26.734 --> 00:00:29.834
-Hey.
-Here's Madeline from E! News.

00:00:29.834 --> 00:00:32.601
-Sally Reed,
how you doing, girl?

00:00:32.601 --> 00:00:33.667
-I'm good.

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:34.901
-Now, the show
takes place in Joplin,

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:35.901
and you were raised there.

00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:36.934
-Mm-hmm. That's right.

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:38.300
-Is that really what
Joplin's like?

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.634
-Well, it's a lot less
freezing on set.

00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:42.834
[ Both laugh ]

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:45.601
Okay, great. Thanks.

00:00:45.601 --> 00:00:47.634
-Please welcome back to the show
our very good friend

00:00:47.634 --> 00:00:49.901
Bill Hader, everybody!

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:52.501
[ Cheers and applause ]
Hello, friend!

00:00:52.501 --> 00:00:53.868
-Hey, buddy.

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:55.434
-How are you?
-How are you, man?

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:57.667
I'm good, man, I'm good.

00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.400
-We've been talking
a great deal via text,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.901
because I'm really obsessed
with this season of the show.

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:04.267
It's a fantastic cast.

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:06.968
And that clip is --
Just to set it up for anybody

00:01:06.968 --> 00:01:11.000
who isn't watching, she has
written and created a show,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.300
and that is a press junket that
people like you have had to do

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.934
over the years when you're in
a movie or for "Barry."

00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:20.501
And is it as much like that as
you have written it in the show?

00:01:20.501 --> 00:01:23.534
-Yes. Yes.

00:01:23.534 --> 00:01:27.334
Press junkets are not fun,
and that was one is based on --

00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:30.167
I was in a movie called
"The Skeleton Twins,"

00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:36.434
and in that movie,
my character attempts suicide.

00:01:36.434 --> 00:01:37.701
Hilarious.

00:01:37.701 --> 00:01:43.734
And I was in Australia,
and the guy --

00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:45.734
I was being interviewed,
and the guy was --

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:51.901
He said, "So, you know,
you try to commit to suicide

00:01:51.901 --> 00:01:56.634
in this film, and, you know,
that's a big epidemic

00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:58.801
here in Australia.

00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:00.767
So can you tell us
a bit about it?

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:03.000
You know,
what you think about suicide."

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.133
And I was like,
"Oh, well, I mean, it's awful,

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:11.901
and, you know, the character,
what he's going through,

00:02:11.901 --> 00:02:13.100
you know, he feels hopeless."

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.067
And then the guy
cut me off and went,

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:16.200
"Uh, we're running out of time.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.601
Real quick,
what do you think about

00:02:17.601 --> 00:02:19.767
Ben Affleck as the new Batman?"

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:21.400
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:28.367
I just was like, "Uh..."

00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:31.367
-Oh, my God.

00:02:31.367 --> 00:02:33.634
That is -- That does make
my next question awkward.

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:35.968
What do you think about
Robert Pattinson as the...

00:02:35.968 --> 00:02:37.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.934 --> 00:02:44.968
Sorry. Once I write it down,
Bill, I have to ask it, so...

00:02:44.968 --> 00:02:47.200
Have you ever --
Like, I would also imagine

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.067
one of the problems is,
you know,

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:51.100
when it's five minutes,
which a lot of these --

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.634
And to, like,
these reporters' defense,

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:55.200
they often only have
five minutes.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.267
-Yes, that's true.

00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:57.400
-So they want to ask --

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.934
You must get
the same questions all the time.

00:02:58.934 --> 00:03:00.067
Have you ever thought of, like,

00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:03.767
making up answers that
weren't true?

00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:06.601
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:06.601 --> 00:03:09.167
Yeah, I mean, you can kind of --
You know what it is?

00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:12.167
You go in these things --
And I can tell you the movie

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:13.534
if it was, like, "It."

00:03:13.534 --> 00:03:16.234
I was in "It Two,"
and everything was like,

00:03:16.234 --> 00:03:18.100
"Did you read the book?

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.934
Did you see --
Have you ever met Stephen King?"

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:24.300
And, you know,
"Did you see the first movie?"

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.701
And, you know, it didn't matter
what language it was in.

00:03:27.701 --> 00:03:29.200
You would go to another country,
and they --

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.901
You didn't even have to
speak the language to be like,

00:03:30.901 --> 00:03:32.234
"I did read the book.

00:03:32.234 --> 00:03:34.234
I have not met Stephen King.

00:03:34.234 --> 00:03:36.334
I did see the first movie."

00:03:36.334 --> 00:03:37.968
But -- I don't know.

00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:39.901
Yeah, I wish I could make
something up.

00:03:39.901 --> 00:03:44.000
Yeah, just be like, "And then,
you know, there was that fire.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.801
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:51.834
And, well,
we all know about the fire."

00:03:51.834 --> 00:03:53.801
-One of the things about "Barry"
that I have been

00:03:53.801 --> 00:03:56.234
talking to you about --
I've seen a few more episodes,

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:57.901
I think, than have aired
at this point.

00:03:57.901 --> 00:03:59.968
There's some
incredible action sequences.

00:03:59.968 --> 00:04:03.701
There's some incredibly
tense, dramatic scenes.

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:05.834
As a director,
have you been lucky enough

00:04:05.834 --> 00:04:07.667
to talk to other directors
over the years?

00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:08.901
Because it does feel like --

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:10.901
I mean, I know you to be
somebody who loves movies,

00:04:10.901 --> 00:04:12.234
and it does seem like
you borrow from

00:04:12.234 --> 00:04:13.868
all your favorites,
stylistically.

00:04:13.868 --> 00:04:16.934
-Yeah. Yeah, I mean --
You know, I mean,

00:04:16.934 --> 00:04:21.067
a lot of credit to Carl Herse,

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:23.267
the D.P. on some of
those episodes.

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:26.634
You know, we work
really close together, and --

00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:32.334
But, yeah, you know, it is neat
to get texts or e-mails

00:04:32.334 --> 00:04:34.200
from people you really admire.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.434
But my problem -- and I don't
know if you relate to this

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:38.868
when we were at "SNL" --
is, like,

00:04:38.868 --> 00:04:40.267
your heroes would come in.

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:44.167
Like, Martin Scorsese came in
to do a bit on "Update,"

00:04:44.167 --> 00:04:46.834
and I -- You know,
someone told me like,

00:04:46.834 --> 00:04:48.200
"Bill, you got to get out of
his dressing room,"

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.067
'cause I was just asking him
like 100 questions.

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:53.501
And, like, I think the minute
I walked in,

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.400
he went, "Uh-oh, geek, uh-oh."

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.234
You know, he's been dealing
with this since like 1975,

00:04:58.234 --> 00:05:00.267
and I'm like,
"Hello, Mr. Scorsese."

00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:02.968
And Steven Spielberg did
a "Laser Cats."

00:05:02.968 --> 00:05:05.067
And, you know, they don't want
to hear, you know --

00:05:05.067 --> 00:05:06.434
-Well, I think
I remember you telling me,

00:05:06.434 --> 00:05:09.567
because I think this really
speaks to the kind of geek

00:05:09.567 --> 00:05:12.434
you are, when Steven Spielberg
showed up at "SNL,"

00:05:12.434 --> 00:05:14.934
tell everybody the movie
you asked him about.

00:05:14.934 --> 00:05:16.934
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:20.367 --> 00:05:24.567
-Everybody here, make your guess
of the Steven Spielberg movie.

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:26.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:30.300
-I said, "How did you get a
specific shot in

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.667
'Sugarland Express'?"

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:37.300
Which is the first feature film
he made before "Jaws."

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:41.133
And he kind of looked at
me like, "Is that even on DVD?"

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:46.634
-You know, he was like,
"I'm not sure I directed that."

00:05:46.634 --> 00:05:49.434
-Yeah, I don't know --
Yeah, and Lorne was like,

00:05:49.434 --> 00:05:52.901
"Bill, just can you please
get off of his lap?

00:05:52.901 --> 00:05:54.934
The man's just trying to eat."

00:05:54.934 --> 00:05:56.601
-I am genuinely curious.

00:05:56.601 --> 00:06:00.100
Because I keep
having friends now who

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:03.033
their kids are teenagers --
and I know your oldest is 12 --

00:06:03.033 --> 00:06:06.934
that they start finding old
"SNLs," like, our-era "SNLs."

00:06:06.934 --> 00:06:10.234
But do your kids -- They're 12,
9, and 7, I think, right?

00:06:10.234 --> 00:06:13.667
-Yeah. Yeah, my daughters --
they like the current season

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:16.100
a lot,
and I will watch it with them.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.400
And then I turn to them and say,

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.000
"You know,
I used to be on this show."

00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:27.734
And they say, "Uh, yeah,
you keep telling us that."

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:34.300
And -- But one night, I did peek
into my daughter's room,

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.300
and she and her friend
had the laptop open

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.767
and they were watching Stefon.

00:06:38.767 --> 00:06:42.133
And, so, they think
you are related to them.

00:06:42.133 --> 00:06:43.801
No.

00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:46.467
No, but they were very confused.

00:06:48.501 --> 00:06:50.334
-Yeah, Stefon's
gonna be a fun one

00:06:50.334 --> 00:06:51.901
to break down
over the years, yeah.

00:06:51.901 --> 00:06:53.267
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:53.267 --> 00:06:56.767
It's like, yeah, that's one for
them and their therapist.

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.534
-You know, you were kind
of known as the impression guy

00:06:59.534 --> 00:07:02.300
at "SNL,"
but you obviously have "Barry"

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.067
and, you know, a lot of other
things right now on your plate.

00:07:05.067 --> 00:07:07.367
And I feel like
there's been a vacancy,

00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:11.934
and I think I'm kind of
the impression guy now.

00:07:11.934 --> 00:07:14.334
-Oh, yeah?
-Yeah.

00:07:14.334 --> 00:07:15.667
Yeah, I do the impressions.

00:07:15.667 --> 00:07:17.067
-Wait. When did you do it --

00:07:17.067 --> 00:07:18.834
Wait, but did you do
impressions on the --

00:07:18.834 --> 00:07:19.868
I don't remember you doing --
-No.

00:07:19.868 --> 00:07:22.534
I think out of --
You know, I think out of

00:07:22.534 --> 00:07:26.901
deference to you and respect,
I kind of let you have that.

00:07:26.901 --> 00:07:30.767
It felt like that was the thing
you needed at the time,

00:07:30.767 --> 00:07:34.467
and then --
But I do the impressions.

00:07:34.467 --> 00:07:38.467
I do the impressions now.
I do them.

00:07:38.467 --> 00:07:42.033
-Alright.
-I do Pacino now. I do it.

00:07:42.033 --> 00:07:43.667
-Well, let's see it.

00:07:44.634 --> 00:07:49.634
-Okay, but before I do it,
don't do it after me.

00:07:49.634 --> 00:07:51.634
[ Laughter ]

00:07:52.934 --> 00:07:54.934
-Let's see it.
-Okay. Alright, ready?

00:07:54.934 --> 00:07:56.701
This has been my Al Pacino.
Ready?

00:07:56.701 --> 00:07:59.934
[ As Pacino ]
I'll see you in the Zoom Room.

00:07:59.934 --> 00:08:02.000
[ Laughter ]

00:08:05.467 --> 00:08:10.067
-I'm so glad I had not seen that
before I did my Al Pacino.

00:08:10.067 --> 00:08:14.234
-Yeah. I should have told you to
turn off the camera first.

00:08:14.234 --> 00:08:16.634
-That was really...

00:08:16.634 --> 00:08:20.067
I had no idea
you had the skill set.

00:08:20.067 --> 00:08:21.367
-Yeah, it's weird.

00:08:21.367 --> 00:08:23.200
It's like once you left the
building, like,

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.133
I feel like all your skills,
like, came --

00:08:26.133 --> 00:08:29.334
like, I inherited them.

00:08:29.334 --> 00:08:30.634
So, just,
if you don't mind, like,

00:08:30.634 --> 00:08:33.167
letting everybody else know,
that's me now.

00:08:33.167 --> 00:08:34.934
-Who else can you do?

00:08:34.934 --> 00:08:37.367
-I can do --
You want to hear Vince Vaughn?

00:08:37.367 --> 00:08:39.567
[ Laughter ]

00:08:39.567 --> 00:08:41.367
Okay, ready?

00:08:41.367 --> 00:08:42.667
Ready? Here's my Vince Vaughn.
Ready?

00:08:42.667 --> 00:08:43.868
[ As Vaughn ]
Buddy, buddy, buddy...

00:08:43.868 --> 00:08:45.968
[ Normal voice ]
Pretty good, right?

00:08:45.968 --> 00:08:49.100
Bill? Bill?
Oh, there he is. He came back.

00:08:50.601 --> 00:08:52.868
-Wait. Do it again.
-Okay, wait.

00:08:52.868 --> 00:08:54.100
And now I'm gonna do
Michael Keaton.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.033
Just ask me any question.

00:08:56.033 --> 00:08:59.267
-Oh. How do I get to 30 Rock?
-Oh, sorry.

00:08:59.267 --> 00:09:01.200
It's gotta be
a "yes"-or-"no" question.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:02.834
-Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:06.834 --> 00:09:09.234
-It's one of my newer ones.

00:09:09.234 --> 00:09:11.667
-Oh, okay.
-Okay, hold on.

00:09:12.567 --> 00:09:14.801
-Am I in 30 Rock?

00:09:16.367 --> 00:09:18.934
-[ As Keaton ] No.

00:09:18.934 --> 00:09:20.634
-That was good.

00:09:20.634 --> 00:09:22.167
-Right?
[ Laughter ]

00:09:26.067 --> 00:09:27.367
See? At first, you had doubts,

00:09:27.367 --> 00:09:28.667
but now you know
I'm the impression guy now.

00:09:28.667 --> 00:09:30.634
-Do your Vince Vaughn again.
Do Vince Vaughn again.

00:09:30.634 --> 00:09:31.701
-Do Vince Vaughn again?

00:09:31.701 --> 00:09:32.734
[ As Vaughn ]
Buddy, buddy, buddy...

00:09:32.734 --> 00:09:34.734
-[ Laughs ]

00:09:38.334 --> 00:09:40.200
-This must be
so weird for you to, like,

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.234
realize the torch
has been passed.

00:09:42.234 --> 00:09:45.367
-I am just so happy
I did not know that

00:09:45.367 --> 00:09:48.434
while you were there or I would
have been super in my head,

00:09:48.434 --> 00:09:50.133
'cause that's really solid.

00:09:50.133 --> 00:09:51.801
-Yeah.
-You're not on "SNL."

00:09:51.801 --> 00:09:53.934
You're just doing these
on your show.

00:09:53.934 --> 00:09:55.267
-Yeah, I'm just
doing them here now.

00:09:57.367 --> 00:10:02.334
Bill, I really --
this season is spectacular,

00:10:02.334 --> 00:10:05.801
and it constantly -- I was a
fan of the first two seasons.

00:10:05.801 --> 00:10:08.868
I'm just blown away by how
you've raised the bar.

00:10:08.868 --> 00:10:10.234
And it's just
so lovely to see you, buddy.

00:10:10.234 --> 00:10:11.234
I love you so much.

00:10:11.234 --> 00:10:13.167
-Thank you, buddy.
Love you, man.

00:10:13.167 --> 00:10:15.133
-Alright, you guys.
That's Bill Hader.

00:10:15.133 --> 00:10:18.167
New episodes of "Barry" air
Sunday nights on HBO

00:10:18.167 --> 00:10:20.367
and are available to stream
anytime on HBO Max.

00:10:20.367 --> 00:10:21.801
We'll be right back
with Minnie Driver.

